WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.260
Well-planned trilogies and intricately connected
film "universes" have revolutionized the idea

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:06.560
of sequels.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.260
Sequels are now highly anticipated and do
quite well at the box office.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.050
Some less successful franchises have a long
history of keepin' on, however, providing

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.230
more content than anyone expected.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.730
Here are some movies with way more sequels
than you thought.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:21.060
The Scorpion King

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.900
After two Indiana Jones-style adventures with
Brendan Fraser and Rachel Weisz, the filmmakers

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.820
of the The Mummy movies realized that the
true future of the franchise lay with Dwayne

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.691
Johnson, who had a supporting role in The
Mummy Returns as Mathayus, the ancient scorpion

00:00:34.691 --> 00:00:35.691
king.

00:00:35.691 --> 00:00:39.680
And so came the third movie in the series,
a prequel-slash-spinoff called The Scorpion

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:40.680
King.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.150
It was a big hit, but Johnson bowed out of
playing Mathayus again; the direct-to-DVD

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:49.240
prequel The Scorpion King 2: Rise of a Warrior
starred an actor and model named Michael Copon.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.570
Days of Our Lives veteran Victor Webster took
over for the next two Scorpion King entries,

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:57.809
The Scorpion King 3: Battle for Redemption
and The Scorpion King 4: Quest for Power.

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:00.460
He was joined by a few other up-and-comers,
as well.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.740
"I am Argomael, noble destroyer."

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:10.940
Air Bud

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:16.359
In 1997, Disney's Budverse started quietly
with the theatrical release of Air Bud.

00:01:16.359 --> 00:01:20.469
It's the charming and unlikely story of a
golden retriever named Buddy that played basketball.

00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:21.799
"Do you like basketball?"

00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:27.029
It brought in a modest $23 million at the
domestic box office off a $3 million budget,

00:01:27.029 --> 00:01:30.859
so a year later dog-sports fans got Air Bud:
Golden Receiver.

00:01:30.859 --> 00:01:32.539
This time the pooch played football.

00:01:32.539 --> 00:01:34.520
"You can play football?"

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:39.310
The movie fumbled at the box office with less
than an $11 million domestic take, but Disney

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:44.069
turned to the direct-to-video circuit to keep
the franchise going…and going.

00:01:44.069 --> 00:01:49.079
In rapid succession, Buddy played soccer in
Air Bud: World Pup, baseball in Air Bud: Seventh

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:53.009
Inning Fetch, and beach volleyball in Air
Bud Spikes Back.

00:01:53.009 --> 00:01:57.590
Then came the next generation: the "Buddies"
movies focused on Buddy's puppies, who anchored

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.219
nine adventures to date.

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:02.950
They've done everything from battling supervillains
to learning the true meaning of Christmas.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:07.389
For those keeping count, that's 13 sequels.

00:02:07.389 --> 00:02:08.389
Death Wish

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:13.709
This 1974 crime thriller starred Charles Bronson
as a regular guy who lays waste to every street

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:15.120
thug he encounters.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.040
It resonated with audiences, providing a romanticized
"solution" to the danger and violence plaguing

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:25.610
city streets in the 1970s, but the inevitable
Death Wish sequels turned into generic action

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:26.610
movies.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.500
Charles Bronson would utter a quip, shoot
a cartoonishly evil thug, and then repeat.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.100
"Do you believe in Jesus?"

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:36.180
"Well, you're going to meet him."

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:41.680
Between 1982 and 1987, Cannon Films churned
out Death Wish 2, Death Wish 3, and Death

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.540
Wish 4: The Crackdown.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.790
The final entry in the increasingly irrelevant
series was the fashion world-centered fourth

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.599
sequel Death Wish V: The Face of Death.

00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:55.969
That came out in 1994, and the series laid
low for awhile...until 2018, when it became

00:02:55.969 --> 00:02:58.640
yet another reboot.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.349
Anaconda

00:03:00.349 --> 00:03:04.730
The 1997 movie Anaconda boasted way too good
of a cast for the kind of movie it was.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:09.560
Owen Wilson, Jennifer Lopez, Ice Cube, and
Eric Stoltz played a documentary crew traveling

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.940
on the Amazon River terrorized by a gigantic
jungle beast.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:18.099
The movie was generic and poorly-reviewed…but
it made $65 million at the box office, so

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:21.829
Anacondas: The Hunt for the Blood Orchid followed
in 2004.

00:03:21.829 --> 00:03:25.790
It was virtually the same movie as Anaconda,
but with a group of researchers in Borneo

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.480
subbing in for the film crew in South America.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.920
That one took in about half of the first movie's
haul, so the series retreated to television.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:38.499
Two made-for-TV sequels followed: Anaconda
3: Offspring in 2008 and Anacondas: Trail

00:03:38.499 --> 00:03:40.540
of Blood in 2009.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:45.670
The series' fourth sequel was a 2015 crossover
with the similar '90s underwater creature

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:51.550
feature Lake Placid, cleverly titled Lake
Placid vs. Anaconda.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:52.819
American Pie

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:57.120
The main characters from American Pie got
four whole movies and a complete plot arc:

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.650
Jim, Stifler, Michelle, and the rest went
to college in American Pie 2, reunited for

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:05.459
American Wedding, and reunited again for American
Reunion.

00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:07.450
But its sequels don't end there.

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.310
Along the way, the "American Pie Presents"
title has been used mainly as a way to brand

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:16.209
raunchy comedies, stuff like American Pie
Presents: Band Camp and American Pie Presents:

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:18.980
Beta House, just to name a few.

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:20.220
Universal Soldier

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.840
Released in 1992, Universal Soldier was a
fine entry in the line-up of Jean-Claude Van

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.330
Damme action movies.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:30.919
In the slick science-fiction thriller, JCVD
plays a fallen soldier who's revived as a

00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:35.229
technologically-enhanced super trooper thanks
to a top-secret military program.

00:04:35.229 --> 00:04:41.210
Van Damme returned for three direct, theatrically-released
sequels: Universal Soldier: The Return, Universal

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.430
Soldier: Regeneration, and Universal Soldier:
Day of Reckoning.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:49.229
That's a lot, but there were even more in
the form of two made-for-TV sequels made in

00:04:49.229 --> 00:04:54.280
between the first two movies: Universal Soldier
II: Brothers in Arms and Universal Soldier

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.199
III: Unfinished Business.

00:04:57.199 --> 00:04:58.699
The Land Before Time

00:04:58.699 --> 00:05:03.780
In 1988, The Land Before Time was a rare animated
hit that wasn't a Disney movie.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:08.500
It brought in a whopping $84 million worldwide
because what's not to like, it's about a bunch

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:12.280
of cute dinosaurs who have big adventures
in prehistoric times.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:16.730
Kids always love dinosaurs, but as they outgrow
their dino phase and grow up, there are always

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:19.740
more dino-cherishing kids coming up behind
them.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:24.550
Producers of The Land Before Time have obliged
with an unrelenting number of sequels.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:30.949
Between 1994 and 2007, 12 more Land Before
Time movies hit home video, along with a reboot

00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:33.750
movie in 2016.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:34.830
Bring It On

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:39.660
The first Bring It On was an amusing, self-effacing
comedy about competitive high school cheerleading.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:43.080
Apparently there's a big demand out there
for movies about high-stakes cheerleading,

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.960
because five more Bring It On movies have
hit home video.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.180
While the first starred Kirsten Dunst and
Gabrielle Union, the others, with titles like

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:54.300
Bring It On Again and Bring it On: In It to
Win It, have starred actresses who were less

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.560
famous or just starting out.

00:05:58.009 --> 00:06:02.560
The most recent iteration is called Bring
It On: Worldwide Cheersmack and is about,

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.159
that's right, a high school cheerleading competition.

00:06:05.159 --> 00:06:10.190
"Three two one bring it on."

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.190
The Pink Panther

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:15.190
The majority of The Pink Panther movies were
directed by Blake Edwards and served as a

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:19.351
vehicle for British comic genius Peter Sellers,
who disappeared into the lead role of the

00:06:19.351 --> 00:06:22.580
clumsy and incompetent French detective Inspector
Clouseau.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:27.960
"There is much more going on here than meets
the ear!"

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:38.100
The Edwards-Sellers team-up, along with an
iconic score by Henry Mancini, and opening

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:42.479
animated sequences featuring the literal Pink
Panther, led to frequent movie magic.

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:45.100
The pair made six Clouseau movies together.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:49.940
After Sellers passed away in 1980, Edwards
tried to reboot the series twice, once with

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:53.830
Roger Moore and once with future Oscar winner
Roberto Benigni.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:58.629
That doesn't even account for the rogue, non-Sellers
Pink Panther movie from 1968 called Inspector

00:06:58.629 --> 00:07:03.439
Clouseau, or, of course, the two rebooted
Pink Panther films starring Steve Martin.

00:07:03.439 --> 00:07:06.000
"I don't even own a mace."

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.379
Planet of the Apes

00:07:07.379 --> 00:07:11.379
Everybody remembers the shocking twist ending
of the first Planet of the Apes movie from

00:07:11.379 --> 00:07:12.889
1968.

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:17.000
Not as many remember that for the first half
of the 1970s, a new Planet of the Apes movie

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.750
hit theaters every summer like clockwork.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:23.690
That's probably because they lacked classic
moments like the ending of the first film,

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.509
or because Charlton Heston didn't return to
ham it up.

00:07:26.509 --> 00:07:31.180
"Take your stinking paws off me you damn dirty
ape."

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:35.599
Each of the four sequels also did worse at
the box office than its predecessor did.

00:07:35.599 --> 00:07:40.550
Add in Tim Burton's 2001 remake and the ongoing
rebooted franchise, and you've got nine total

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:42.470
Planet of the Apes movies.

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:43.470
Thanks for watching!

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.639
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:47.710
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

